Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Window Twelve Never Prints the Fare
- Story & Context: During the first fare-table rehearsal, Neema finds that service window twelve still contains a jammed manual ticket printer. Each pull of the handle produces two fast mechanical knocks and one softer return click. She maps the three-part pattern into the singeli grid but leaves the timing slightly uneven. Amani answers every second printer cycle with one zeze slide that lasts across eight bars. Idris uses short prepared-harmonium breaths only when the printer sound disappears, while Ruth enters after the twelfth cycle with a contrabass-recorder fundamental just above the electronic bass. Midway through the track, the printer handle sticks and the mechanical pattern vanishes. The band preserves its empty spaces for sixteen bars before allowing a new percussion cell to occupy them.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Old ticket window marked 12 with a jammed manual printer, red fare stamps scattered on terrazzo, zeze bow in the foreground, compact drum machine on the counter, prepared harmonium and contrabass recorder in soft focus. Spoken fragments limited to “window twelve,” “leave the jam,” “do not replace it,” and “sixteen more.”

Track 2: Bellows Close at Two Hundred BPM
- Story & Context: Idris attempts to match a 200 BPM singeli passage by pumping the harmonium faster than is physically comfortable. Instead of sustaining the effort, he discovers that closing the bellows at irregular points creates stronger rhythmic accents than trying to play continuously. Neema builds the arrangement around those closures. Amani performs slow zeze dyads that ignore the drum subdivisions, while Ruth uses only air and key noise for the first half of the track. At the peak, the kick pattern becomes denser but the harmonium plays less often, creating a paradoxical sense of increased speed through reduced harmonic activity. The bridge drops to zeze scrape, one harmonium exhale, and contrabass-recorder breath before the full grid snaps back without a riser.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Close view of portable harmonium bellows half-closed under a musician’s hand, blurred fare numbers behind it, zeze string and bow hair crossing one corner, contrabass-recorder keys catching warm streetlight. Spoken fragments limited to “close at two hundred,” “do not chase the drums,” “less now,” and “open once.”

Track 3: The Queue Rail Keeps the Last Pulse
- Story & Context: A loose chrome queue rail near the hall entrance rattles whenever buses pass on the road outside. Neema records one vibration and places it after the final snare of every fourth phrase. Amani begins ending each zeze gesture just before the rail sound, leaving only string resonance beneath the metal tremor. Idris uses damped harmonium notes as short midrange knocks, while Ruth alternates between underblown low tones and silence. In the final minute the electronic bass is removed, then the main kick disappears, but the rail vibration, zeze bridge noise, harmonium air, and recorder breath continue to imply the original tempo. The EP ends with a real passing bus shaking the rail after the machines have stopped.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Rusted chrome queue rail vibrating beside the open doorway of an abandoned ferry-ticket hall, blurred bus lights outside, zeze and prepared harmonium deeper in the room, contrabass recorder silhouette near faded fare boards. Spoken fragments limited to “after the snare,” “leave the rail,” “no bass now,” and “wait for the bus.”

